#|
Linux/Android relative-axis example for devices that expose extra controls.
These names correspond to real evdev relative codes, not invented wheel IDs.
Check the device's EV_REL capabilities: an EV_ABS axis is not transformed.

- rx/ry add pointer movement.
- z and dial add vertical scrolling; one dial step is one notch.
- rz adds horizontal scrolling.
- misc is routed to native Z output for an application that understands it.

This uses more input channels than output channels. Missing input channels
contribute zero. Desktop cursors do not gain a third spatial coordinate:
native Z output is for a consumer that understands REL_Z.
See docs/config.adoc, mouse-axis-map.
|#

(defcfg
  process-unmapped-keys no
  ;; Narrow device selection using linux-dev or linux-dev-names-include as needed.
  mouse-axis-map (
    (x (x 1) (rx 0.5))
    (y (y 1) (ry 0.5))
    (v (v 1) (z -12) (dial 120))
    (h (h 1) (rz 12))
    (z (misc 1))
    (rx)
    (ry)
    (rz)
    (dial)
    (misc)
  )
)
(defsrc)
(deflayer base)
